  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p27-q7-stem-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There are 5 students, and each enters one of two distinct classrooms, ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "A",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " or ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "B",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many ways are there for them to enter the classrooms?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2^5=32$ ways.",
          "worked": "Each of the 5 students has 2 choices, $A$ or $B$.\n$$2\\times2\\times2\\times2\\times2=2^5=32\\text{ ways}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If all students enter room $B$, how many ways can this be done?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "1 way.",
          "worked": "Every student's classroom is fixed as $B$, so there is exactly 1 assignment.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q7-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "If only one student is to enter room $A$, how many ways can this be done?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{5}{1}=5$ ways.",
          "worked": "Choose the one student who enters $A$. The other four must enter $B$.\n$$\\binom{5}{1}=5\\text{ ways}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q7-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "If there are to be 2 students to enter room $A$, how many ways can this be done?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{5}{2}=10$ ways.",
          "worked": "Choose the two students who enter $A$. The other three must enter $B$.\n$$\\binom{5}{2}=\\frac{5\\times4}{2\\times1}=10\\text{ ways}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q7-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "By using a combinatorial argument, show that\n$$\\binom{5}{0}+\\binom{5}{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{5}{5}=2^5.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{5}{0}+\\binom{5}{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{5}{5}=2^5=32$.\n\nCount classroom assignments by the number entering $A$, or by each student's two choices.",
          "worked": "If exactly $k$ students enter $A$, choose them in $\\binom{5}{k}$ ways; all remaining students enter $B$. The mutually exclusive cases $k=0,1,\\ldots,5$ cover every classroom assignment, so their total is\n$$\\binom{5}{0}+\\binom{5}{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{5}{5}.$$\nEach of the 5 students also has 2 independent choices, giving $2^5$ assignments. Both methods count the same assignments, so\n$$\\binom{5}{0}+\\binom{5}{1}+\\cdots+\\binom{5}{5}=2^5.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
